// **`print` with `%` placeholders over a `..Any` variadic** (ADR-0189).
//
// # Why this file exists
//
// Until this wave the library could print a string and one non-negative integer, so a program that
// wanted to show a float, a negative number, a `bool` or a struct had nothing to call. `print_int`
// could not even render `S64_MIN`, because it negated first.
//
// What is exercised here is the *composition*: `print` is a `..Any` variadic (ADR-0138/0139 built the
// packing, ADR-0076 the erasure), each argument is discriminated at run time by its own `Type_Info`
// (ADR-0075), and the placeholder scan decides where each lands. Every branch of `format_any` that a
// corpus program can observe is taken below.
//
// # What is deliberately absent, and why
//
// **A pointer.** `%` on a pointer prints its address, and the bytecode VM's addresses are
// region-relative while a native binary's are real — so the two engines legitimately disagree and the
// value has no home in `valid/`, whose premise is that they agree. Verified by hand in both.
//
// **A view and an enum member's name.** Both print `<view>` / an ordinal, and ADR-0189 §6 records the
// one root cause: a `Type_Info` field carrying a type **id** cannot be resolved back to a
// `*Type_Info`, so nothing can recurse into an element or look a member up. Asserted below as it is
// rather than left out, so the day that lifts, this file's expectation changes visibly.
//
// The exit code is a checksum of what printed, so a wrong rendering fails the run rather than only
// looking wrong in a log nobody reads.

#import "Basic";

Point :: struct {
    x: s64;
    y: s64;
}

Colour :: enum {
    RED;
    GREEN;
    BLUE;
}

main :: () {
    total := 0;

    // A format string with no placeholder at all, and one that is only placeholders.
    total = total + print("plain\n");
    total = total + print("%-%\n", 1, 2);

    // Every integer width, signed and unsigned, including the two that a naive renderer gets wrong:
    // `S64_MIN` cannot be negated, and `U64_MAX` does not fit in an `s64`.
    print("% % % %\n", cast(s8, -128), cast(s16, -32768), cast(s32, -2147483648), -9223372036854775807 - 1);
    // `U64_MAX` through a typed local: a bare literal in a variadic takes `s64` from its context and
    // does not fit, which is E0204 working rather than a gap.
    big: u64 = 18446744073709551615;
    print("% %\n", cast(u8, 255), big);

    // A `bool`, a `string`, a `float64` and a `float32` widened to one.
    print("% % % %\n", true, false, "text", 1.5);
    print("%\n", cast(float32, -0.25));

    // A struct, one level deep, by field name.
    p: Point;
    p.x = 3;
    p.y = 4;
    print("%\n", p);

    // A fixed-size array, whose stride comes from `size / count`.
    a: [3]s64;
    a[0] = 10;
    a[1] = 20;
    a[2] = 30;
    print("%\n", a);

    // An escaped `%%`, and the two wrong-argument-count diagnostics. None of the three is an error:
    // a `print` that refuses to print is worse than one that says what it was given.
    print("100%% done\n");
    print("short %\n");
    print("long\n", 9);

    // The two shapes ADR-0189 §6 leaves as they are.
    c := Colour.BLUE;
    print("%\n", c);
    v := view(*a[0], 3);
    print("%\n", v);

    // `plain\n` is 6 bytes; `1-2\n` is 4. A rendering that drops or adds a byte changes this.
    if total != 10 {
        exit(1);
    }
    exit(64);
}
